  -> release window 3.25..10.50 deg into the index; spread 7.25 deg across D11..D13, 3.75 deg at D13 alone (r9 published 1.00 deg from a radial-offset construction and the r3 critic measured 7.25/3.75 independently -- these now agree). Separation at release 6.690..10.701 mm. B7.1 (support retained until |separation| <= 1.0 mm) FAILS: the pellet leaves while the disc is still moving. Lateral velocity at the PCD = omega x 32 mm = 0.126 m/s at the 225 deg/s mean index rate and 0.25 m/s at a 450 deg/s peak [D, profile assumption carried from r6, not re-derived]. THIS IS A RECORDED PLATEAU, not a closure -- see BUILD-NOTES-r2 B7 and the corrected README/DESIGN text. Park retention is unaffected and is re-printed above.

EXIT KINEMATICS: the index is TWO-PHASE -- park(+22.5 deg) -> index 22.5 deg to the port -> DWELL -> index 22.5 deg to the next park. (a) release happens 3.25..10.50 deg INTO the first index, i.e. before the dwell begins, through a partial lune -- NOT from a stationary, concentric pocket (measured above); (b) the drop window is still a DWELL parameter, not a race -- fall time to clear the 14.5 mm pocket is 54 ms, so the contract dwell is 150 ms (2.8x); (c) the dwell is what guarantees the pellet is clear of the disc before the second index, and that part of the r6 argument survives.
exit drop line: straight vertical at (32, 0) -> 32 mm from the airframe centreline; chute bore Dia22 gives 4.5 mm of radial clearance around a worst-case pellet
IR beam at Z=-395.2 (40 mm below plate); v at beam = 0.89 m/s; occlusion ~17 ms (12 mm pellet + 3 mm beam); sensor faces recessed 5.0 mm from bore
chute ID 22.0 < 2xD: two-body arch needs two pellets in the chute; free-fall transit 101 ms << the two-phase cycle -> temporally excluded (rebuttal held from r2)
INTERFACE CONSTRAINT (r6, replaces r4's '>=150 ms index period'): the ICD command contract is INDEX 22.5 deg -> DWELL >= 150 ms -> INDEX 22.5 deg -> park; one pellet per 45 deg, <= 3 pellets/s. The dwell is the only timing the count loop depends on and it is a firmware constant, not a kinematic race.
usable hopper volume (to fill line -248.6): 962 cm3
capacity: worst-case barrel packing 421 pellets (497 g); sphere basis 586 (691 g); requirement >=250 -> PASS (1.68x)
421-pellet fill line (raised rib) at Z=-249.0 (10.5 mm below the lid): volume below the line 954 cm3 = 419 pellets worst-case (target 421) / 582 sphere-basis
250-pellet fill line (raised rib) at Z=-274.4 (35.9 mm below the lid): volume below the line 564 cm3 = 248 pellets worst-case (target 250) / 344 sphere-basis
FILL-LINE RULE (r6): the MAX rib is the VOLUMETRIC brim line (421 pellets, 497 g). CONTEXT exempts pellet mass above the 250 baseline from the 1.5 kg ceiling, and the ceiling case is therefore the 250 load. For transparency the NO-EXEMPTION limits are printed too: on the quadruple-conservative ledger (CF-PETG 1.31 g/cm3, stepper 380 g gross, 100% infill, +10%) empty = 1334.6 g -> only 123 pellets, which is BELOW the 250 hard minimum: that reading is infeasible with a geared drive and r6 says so instead of quietly moving a rib. On the measured-infill ledger the same strict rule gives ~279 pellets. r5's rib was 393 on a 190 g direct drive.
SERVICE DRAIN: pellets below the funnel outlet + one pellet layer (z<-313.2) = 114 cm3 = ~50 pellets. That is the residue that can fall when the cartridge is dropped AFTER the documented drain (QR the payload, invert, remove the fill cap, pour back into the tub) -- not the 421-pellet load.

